1
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,540
- Hello.
- I'm sorry.

2
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:14,090
What? Why?

3
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:19,440
I thought you would accuse me of slacking.

4
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:21,840
No, of course not.

5
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:25,490
Taking breaks is important.

6
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:29,420
What were you up to?

7
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,930
A flowerbed, here?

8
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:38,640
- I never noticed.
- Yes, well,

9
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,990
I saw it from the music room.

10
00:00:41,990 --> 00:00:44,920
My guess is that the janitor planted these.

11
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,650
By the way, Ms. Nashiki.

12
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:49,530
What club activity do you supervise?

13
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,330
I manage the chorus club.

14
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:53,630
The other clubs are a bit much for me.

15
00:00:53,630 --> 00:00:57,080
I see. Chorus, huh?

16
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:00,460
Must be nice,
not having to attend sports matches.

17
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,130
We have recitals instead.

18
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:06,550
- Right.
- Yes.

19
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:08,200
Sorry.

20
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:14,300
<i>♪ Dost we dance or sing? ♪</i>

21
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,190
<i>♪ Or do we come to see
Tokubei's wife on the isle? ♪</i>

22
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:24,440
<i>♪ Three and a four, and a three, and a four ♪</i>

23
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:27,660
That's our current song.

24
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:32,550
What a strange tune.

25
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:34,030
I agree.

26
00:01:35,630 --> 00:01:39,780
<i>♪ Dost we dance or sing? ♪</i>

27
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:42,800
<i>♪ Or do we come to see
Tokubei's wife on the isle? ♪</i>

28
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,790
<i>♪ Three and a four, and a three, and a four ♪</i>

29
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:50,910
<i>♪ Dost we dance or sing? ♪</i>

30
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:53,060
Sir?

31
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,410
Strange guy.

32
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:02,330
Get off, this is our stop.

33
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,210
Thank you.

34
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:19,400
Where is this?

35
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:23,230
A castle?

36
00:02:23,230 --> 00:02:27,370
This is no castle. Come on...

37
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:29,730
- Ow...
- <i>I'm an unlucky guy.</i>

38
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:32,780
<i>I'm confident I'm the unluckiest dude around.</i>

39
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:34,250
What the...

40
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:37,490
What a beautiful orb.

41
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:40,230
Is it agate or quartz?

42
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:45,490
No! You'll break...

43
00:02:49,910 --> 00:02:51,280
Let's go.

44
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,160
Hold on a second.

45
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:55,570
The hell is that? A mechanical orb?

46
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:58,340
No, it's a child's forgotten toy.
We'll leave it here.

47
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,990
Just one more time, please?

48
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:02,780
That bounced like crazy!

49
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:06,290
Keep your voice down! It's night time.

50
00:03:10,370 --> 00:03:15,120
<i>Not only am I unlucky,
but now I've got some insane baggage.</i>

51
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:17,770
Ms. Nashiki, your hand.

52
00:03:27,030 --> 00:03:28,200
Ms. Nashiki!

53
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:30,540
Who's this "Ms. Nashiki"
you keep talking about?

54
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:32,950
I'm Iroha, daughter to the Tokubei Couriers.

55
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:34,370
I have no surname!

56
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:38,990
I'll become the teacher she wanted to be.

57
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:42,170
This is awful.

58
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:43,620
What the heck?

59
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,100
This is awesome!

60
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,910
Like cherry blossom colored snowflakes!

61
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:54,830
<i>Will she change my fate? Or...</i>

62
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:03,980
<i>Yeah, not happening.</i>

63
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,790
Shush! Be quiet!

64
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:08,660
- Come on.
- Yeah.

65
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:10,460
Leave it behind.

66
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:13,580
We're heading in.

67
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:26,030
[Episode 2]

68
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:35,990
You damned specter.

69
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:37,920
I will end you where you stand!

70
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:39,680
My words exactly.

71
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:43,460
For I have returned as a wandering phantom.

72
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:01,620
What the heck? I almost cleared that stage!

73
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,420
- You're the worst.
- Saya.

74
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,840
I told you no playing games all day.

75
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:08,080
Busybody.

76
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,620
- Uh, who's this?
- Don't mind her.

77
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:14,900
Of course I'm going to mind.

78
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:17,030
- Who is she?
- Hey.

79
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:18,430
Those people...

80
00:05:18,430 --> 00:05:21,500
You've never brought over a guest.

81
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:23,090
- Is she your girlfriend?
- No.

82
00:05:23,090 --> 00:05:26,020
Hey, where did those people go?

83
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:27,580
Hello?

84
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:29,830
Huh?

85
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:35,020
I died.

86
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:36,740
[GAME OVER]

87
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:38,620
She died?

88
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:41,660
Damn samurai.

89
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:44,740
Always desecrating people's lives.

90
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:47,640
- How do I get in here?
- Woah...

91
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:49,450
- Hey!
- Is she okay in the head?

92
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:51,180
- Can you hear me?
- That's enough.

93
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:54,290
Anyone? Help!

94
00:05:54,290 --> 00:05:56,870
Forget all that. No one is dead.

95
00:05:56,870 --> 00:05:57,970
Huh?

96
00:05:57,970 --> 00:05:59,190
Let me introduce you two.

97
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:01,730
This is Iroha Nashiki.
She'll be staying at our place.

98
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,330
What? Why?

99
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:04,800
And this is my younger sister, Saya.

100
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,710
Your younger sister?

101
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:11,820
What an absolute doll.

102
00:06:11,820 --> 00:06:13,240
"D-Doll"?

103
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:15,100
Don't mind her language.

104
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:19,640
Hey. I said, hey!

105
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,860
You're in there, aren't you? Hey!

106
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:24,770
You alright?

107
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:29,960
- Hello?
- Ikuru.

108
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:31,920
Ikuru!

109
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,330
Can I get an explanation?

110
00:06:34,330 --> 00:06:37,730
- It'll get a bit long...
- Hey.

111
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:39,750
Where is this?

112
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:41,480
Huh?

113
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:43,210
What do you mean, "where"?

114
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:45,980
This place, it looks like my village.

115
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:48,280
Well...

116
00:06:56,190 --> 00:06:57,920
Alright.

117
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:00,640
- Follow me.
- Right.

118
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:02,310
This is the washroom.

119
00:07:02,310 --> 00:07:03,890
Okay.

120
00:07:03,890 --> 00:07:06,100
I'll leave out a towel for you.

121
00:07:07,990 --> 00:07:09,970
Missus Saya...

122
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:11,640
Yeah?

123
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:13,250
This thing...

124
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:15,800
Our mirror?

125
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:20,820
She really looks exactly like me.

126
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:22,720
Well, it is your own face.

127
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:27,210
I've never seen it so clearly before.

128
00:07:27,210 --> 00:07:32,600
I only see it when I go to the well,
or the hairdresser.

129
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:40,060
It sure does feel strange,
not having my hair up.

130
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:41,630
Huh? R-Right...

131
00:07:44,910 --> 00:07:47,050
- Anyway, here.
- Thanks.

132
00:07:47,050 --> 00:07:49,690
Huh? The hell is this?

133
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:52,110
It's so fluffy!

134
00:07:52,110 --> 00:07:54,390
Hey! Hey!

135
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:56,640
What is this? It's so...

136
00:07:57,740 --> 00:07:59,680
What're you doing?

137
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:06,440
Here you go.

138
00:08:08,150 --> 00:08:11,330
Did you make this?

139
00:08:11,330 --> 00:08:12,580
Yeah.

140
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:14,460
In that short amount of time?

141
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:20,090
Thank you.

142
00:08:20,090 --> 00:08:22,010
No problem.

143
00:08:47,910 --> 00:08:49,700
You...

144
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:51,730
I mean.

145
00:08:51,730 --> 00:08:53,860
Master Ichinose.

146
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:57,790
To think you had such talents.

147
00:09:01,190 --> 00:09:03,520
- Ikuru.
- I know.

148
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,410
I'll explain after I eat.

149
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:09,270
This ain't soba, or udon.

150
00:09:09,270 --> 00:09:13,750
Hell, I bet not even the shogun
has eaten something like this!

151
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:16,730
But to see a man,

152
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:20,090
standing in the kitchen?

153
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:23,340
That's nothing unheard of.

154
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:24,380
What?

155
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:27,680
Yeah, anyone can whip up something like this.

156
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:31,370
Is that why women can be teachers too?

157
00:09:31,370 --> 00:09:32,740
Of course.

158
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:34,730
No one's restricted to a job.

159
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:36,370
That's nothing to take for granted.

160
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:39,200
To be able to choose a job...

161
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:42,400
Normally, your job is dictated
by family lineage.

162
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:46,370
Samurai will be samurai.
Commoners will be commoners.

163
00:09:46,370 --> 00:09:48,640
Since my family runs a courier business,

164
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:53,050
my father was disappointed
to have a baby girl.

165
00:09:54,370 --> 00:09:56,950
Who is Iroha, really?

166
00:09:56,950 --> 00:09:59,540
She speaks like an Edo era NPC.

167
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:04,350
Right. I've never heard of this "Edo era",

168
00:10:04,350 --> 00:10:07,030
but where I was from, it was Jokyo 2 (1685).

169
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:10,410
- Jokyo 2?
- Jokyo 2...

170
00:10:10,410 --> 00:10:12,460
That was...

171
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:13,670
<i>No way.</i>

172
00:10:13,670 --> 00:10:15,900
She's a time traveler?

173
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:19,830
- So next thing you knew, you were here?
- Yeah.

174
00:10:19,830 --> 00:10:22,690
Then where's the original Iroha Nashiki?

175
00:10:22,690 --> 00:10:25,860
- Who knows.
- Seriously?

176
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:27,740
Saya, grab the cups for me.

177
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:30,370
Ikuru! This is no time for washing dishes!

178
00:10:30,370 --> 00:10:32,580
Someone has to clean up.

179
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:35,860
Hold on. Let me get the facts straight.

180
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:39,160
First, what triggered her time traveling?

181
00:10:39,910 --> 00:10:42,130
<i>Was it the comet tonight?</i>

182
00:10:42,930 --> 00:10:44,640
Okay, theory one.

183
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:48,860
Modern Iroha and Edo Iroha swapped places.

184
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:50,110
Theory two.

185
00:10:50,110 --> 00:10:53,610
Edo Iroha is Modern Iroha's ancestor,
and they synced up.

186
00:10:53,610 --> 00:10:55,350
"Sync"?

187
00:10:55,350 --> 00:11:01,790
Theory three.
Modern Iroha's life force was weakened,

188
00:11:01,790 --> 00:11:03,270
so Edo Iroha took over.

189
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:04,530
Took over?

190
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:09,030
That makes me sound like a ghost...

191
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:10,340
Just so we're sure,

192
00:11:10,340 --> 00:11:13,860
Modern Iroha isn't dead or anything, right?

193
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:16,090
Of course not...

194
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:21,640
Of course...

195
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,640
Not...

196
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:26,230
What if I were a ghost?

197
00:11:26,230 --> 00:11:32,460
What if I committed murder?

198
00:11:33,350 --> 00:11:35,250
Get a grip!

199
00:11:35,250 --> 00:11:36,630
- Ikuru.
- What?

200
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:38,180
You too, Iroha.

201
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:39,810
- Huh?
- Stand up. Come on.

202
00:11:39,810 --> 00:11:40,810
Saya!

203
00:11:40,810 --> 00:11:42,920
You traveled through time.

204
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:46,540
Meaning only your soul
came to this modern era.

205
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:49,770
Let's blame it on a bug
in the space-time continuum.

206
00:11:49,770 --> 00:11:51,430
A bug?

207
00:11:51,430 --> 00:11:54,030
In other words,
you'll find your way home eventually.

208
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:57,730
Realistically speaking, that's not possible.

209
00:11:57,730 --> 00:12:00,250
I'd rather blame it all on stress.

210
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:02,850
Face the facts, Ikuru.

211
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:06,240
You've watched "Back to the Future".
"The Girl Who Leapt Through Time"?

212
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:07,880
Those are both works of fiction.

213
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:10,670
You'll be able to go home, promise.

214
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:12,140
Really?

215
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:14,200
You can't be absolutely certain.

216
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,740
The last time we saw that comet
was 341 years ago.

217
00:12:16,740 --> 00:12:20,090
If that was the trigger,
when will it happen next?

218
00:12:20,890 --> 00:12:24,170
So, this "comet".

219
00:12:24,170 --> 00:12:26,880
Even if it doesn't come,

220
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:31,260
maybe I'll be pushed out
if this girl starts wanting to live?

221
00:12:32,530 --> 00:12:34,030
I mean,

222
00:12:34,030 --> 00:12:38,480
this body is her's, right?

223
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:41,560
So if her own soul returns...

224
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,120
You make a point.

225
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:46,250
If Modern Iroha is only unconscious,

226
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:49,400
then she'll likely revive
once her vitality is restored.

227
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:50,590
We have no time.

228
00:12:50,590 --> 00:12:52,850
Once Golden Week is over,
school is back in session.

229
00:12:52,850 --> 00:12:55,800
Let's hope she comes back
after a night's rest.

230
00:12:55,800 --> 00:13:01,080
<i>♪ Dost we dance or sing? ♪</i>

231
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:06,070
- Ikuru.
- What?

232
00:13:06,070 --> 00:13:08,510
Did you like Ms. Nashiki?

233
00:13:08,510 --> 00:13:09,700
- What?
- Is that true?

234
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:11,650
No! No, no, no!

235
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:14,520
I barely even spoke to her!

236
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:17,190
Aw, boring.

237
00:13:17,190 --> 00:13:19,370
Boring.

238
00:13:19,370 --> 00:13:22,000
[Iroha Nashiki]

239
00:13:31,690 --> 00:13:34,060
That looks great on you, Ms. Iroha.

240
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:36,420
I'm glad Mom's old yukata fit you.

241
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:38,450
Thanks.

242
00:13:39,710 --> 00:13:41,990
What? Woah! What's that?

243
00:13:41,990 --> 00:13:43,590
What is it?

244
00:13:43,590 --> 00:13:45,850
- Turn around.
- Okay?

245
00:13:45,850 --> 00:13:47,690
Huh?

246
00:13:47,690 --> 00:13:49,260
Okay, ready?

247
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,380
It's warm!

248
00:13:55,380 --> 00:13:58,790
- Right?
- I get it now.

249
00:13:59,550 --> 00:14:03,690
What will you do
if Ms. Nashiki doesn't return?

250
00:14:03,690 --> 00:14:05,920
Will you be a teacher in her stead?

251
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,970
Yeah, that's the plan.

252
00:14:09,630 --> 00:14:10,970
Right, the textbooks.

253
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:13,860
I borrowed her textbooks.

254
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:17,470
So long as I have these...

255
00:14:17,470 --> 00:14:19,340
[Circles and spheres]

256
00:14:29,350 --> 00:14:30,590
[Mr. Sun's Soup]

257
00:14:30,590 --> 00:14:32,390
I can't read a word.

258
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:35,930
Missus Saya.

259
00:14:35,930 --> 00:14:38,620
May I ask you to teach me?

260
00:14:40,070 --> 00:14:41,810
Alright.

261
00:14:41,810 --> 00:14:44,120
Let's cram this information into your noggin.

262
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:45,660
Yes, ma'am!

263
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:51,020
[Iroha Nashiki, Accident]

264
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:00,520
Ms. Nashiki.

265
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:05,070
A parent called requesting to reschedule
their parent-teacher conference.

266
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:10,130
I already did, three times.

267
00:15:10,130 --> 00:15:13,680
I suppose it happens.
Especially when both parents are working.

268
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:17,500
I had them swap time slots
with another family.

269
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:21,270
Sometimes the changes are a domino effect.

270
00:15:21,270 --> 00:15:23,310
What an absolute pain.

271
00:15:24,670 --> 00:15:27,640
Huh? Are you alright?

272
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:29,320
It's just a rescheduling, right?

273
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:30,860
- Are you okay?
- Ms. Nashiki?

274
00:15:30,860 --> 00:15:33,390
- I'm sorry.
- Are you feeling dizzy?

275
00:15:33,390 --> 00:15:34,810
Excuse us.

276
00:15:34,810 --> 00:15:37,160
- Huh? Where's Morikawa?
- We're almost there.

277
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:38,570
- Not here?
- They must've left.

278
00:15:38,570 --> 00:15:40,300
I see.

279
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:41,560
Let's have you lie down first.

280
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:42,800
Okay.

281
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:46,150
<i>Ms. Sakurai.
To the principal's office, please.</i>

282
00:15:46,150 --> 00:15:47,460
- I'm being called.
- I'll take over.

283
00:15:47,460 --> 00:15:48,750
Thank you.

284
00:15:48,750 --> 00:15:51,380
- Go on, you're almost there.
- Sorry.

285
00:15:51,380 --> 00:15:53,230
And you, try not to touch her.

286
00:15:53,230 --> 00:15:54,360
- Yes, ma'am.
- Thank you.

287
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:56,320
Ms. Nashiki, take my arm.

288
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:58,290
I'm fine.

289
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:01,860
- Thank you.
- Here.

290
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:08,590
- I'll help.
- Thank you.

291
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:15,010
The medicine...

292
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:19,900
Which one is the right one?

293
00:16:20,770 --> 00:16:24,910
I already took medicine
for my stomach earlier.

294
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:27,390
Sorry.

295
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:37,730
When did this start?

296
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:43,450
It's only been two weeks
since the school year began.

297
00:16:47,330 --> 00:16:48,840
I'm sorry.

298
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:50,580
I'm not accusing you.

299
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:53,550
But you shouldn't push yourself.

300
00:16:53,550 --> 00:16:57,220
I won't cause trouble.

301
00:16:58,670 --> 00:17:00,320
Should I tell the principal?

302
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:03,530
So we have back-up if something happens...

303
00:17:05,390 --> 00:17:10,050
I won't ever cause trouble again.

304
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:15,230
Okay.

305
00:17:16,250 --> 00:17:18,640
Thank you.

306
00:17:40,650 --> 00:17:43,200
Ms. Iroha fell asleep.

307
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:47,780
What're you going to do about her?

308
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:54,530
She has a house, and an ID at the very least.

309
00:17:54,530 --> 00:17:57,160
Once she's settled, I'll have her leave.

310
00:17:57,160 --> 00:17:58,120
How cold.

311
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:00,480
It's not like I can do any more for her.

312
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:02,290
Ikuru, are you serious?

313
00:18:02,290 --> 00:18:05,340
Do you really think she can teach
modern elementary schoolers?

314
00:18:05,340 --> 00:18:08,360
I guess she'll have to resign.

315
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:09,510
- Ikuru!
- Woah!

316
00:18:09,510 --> 00:18:13,110
That's horrible!
You're going to just let her fail?

317
00:18:13,110 --> 00:18:16,220
She can't do it, and that's a fact.

318
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,410
But she wants to try.

319
00:18:18,410 --> 00:18:19,940
Maybe then she'll give up.

320
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:21,800
Irresponsible.

321
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:23,420
What, you think I have time for...

322
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:26,480
Coward. Give her a hand!
Maybe it'll work out!

323
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:29,420
If Ms. Iroha can't go back,
she has to live here, you know?

324
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:33,220
That goes for all of us, you and me included.

325
00:18:34,070 --> 00:18:36,440
Then tell me, why don't you go to school?

326
00:18:36,440 --> 00:18:38,510
Why do you refuse to attend?

327
00:18:38,510 --> 00:18:40,870
We're not talking about me right now.

328
00:18:40,870 --> 00:18:45,140
You ran, because reality
didn't live up to your ideals.

329
00:18:45,140 --> 00:18:47,000
Everyone's in a similar boat.

330
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,190
To make your ideals real,
they first have to be possible.

331
00:18:49,190 --> 00:18:51,580
And if they're not,
we either give up, or quit.

332
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:54,330
In other words...

333
00:18:55,860 --> 00:18:58,360
You're going to abandon her?

334
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:00,210
You're the worst.

335
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,640
<i>That's right.</i>

336
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:07,000
<i>People everywhere
are suffering to some degree.</i>

337
00:19:09,970 --> 00:19:13,790
<i>We have no time to spare
on the pain of others.</i>

338
00:19:33,670 --> 00:19:36,280
So it wasn't a dream.

339
00:19:40,590 --> 00:19:41,990
You're heading out?

340
00:19:41,990 --> 00:19:43,590
What about breakfast?

341
00:19:43,590 --> 00:19:45,090
I'll eat out today.

342
00:19:45,090 --> 00:19:47,840
At least bring a rice ball with you.

343
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:50,420
Hold on, I'll cook some rice.

344
00:19:51,020 --> 00:19:54,270
I'm fine. You don't even know
how to use the rice cooker.

345
00:19:54,270 --> 00:19:55,720
Then I'll learn.

346
00:19:55,720 --> 00:19:59,320
I can't have you
setting fires left and right.

347
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:04,170
Sorry. I'm running late.

348
00:20:04,170 --> 00:20:05,860
Hey.

349
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:10,990
<i>This is fine.</i>

350
00:20:10,990 --> 00:20:15,200
<i>I decided my motto, didn't I?</i>

351
00:20:19,310 --> 00:20:23,830
[Not my problem]

352
00:20:23,830 --> 00:20:25,030
"Not my problem"!

353
00:20:25,030 --> 00:20:27,610
[Not my problem]

354
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:30,410
<i>Please recharge.</i>

355
00:20:30,410 --> 00:20:32,370
- Who?
- <i>To set back on standby,</i>

356
00:20:32,370 --> 00:20:36,090
- What the...
- <i>please press the start button.</i>

357
00:20:36,990 --> 00:20:39,410
The hell?

358
00:20:41,010 --> 00:20:43,460
Their mother?

359
00:21:04,060 --> 00:21:07,570
At least the sun is still the same.

360
00:21:09,040 --> 00:21:11,450
People are living their lives.

361
00:21:11,450 --> 00:21:13,840
There are houses, and towns, too.

362
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:15,890
Nothing has changed.

363
00:21:21,650 --> 00:21:24,700
Mount Fuji!

364
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:35,280
[May 7th, 7AM]

365
00:21:35,280 --> 00:21:37,730
Alright.

366
00:21:42,060 --> 00:21:43,840
See you later.

367
00:21:49,570 --> 00:21:52,890
Good morning, Mr. Ichinose.

368
00:21:52,890 --> 00:21:55,670
Huh? Ms. Nashiki?

369
00:21:55,670 --> 00:21:57,330
You're back?

370
00:22:00,980 --> 00:22:02,860
Missus Saya... I mean,

371
00:22:02,860 --> 00:22:06,950
Saya helped me practice.

372
00:22:08,030 --> 00:22:13,290
My name is Iroha Nashiki... Got that?

373
00:22:13,290 --> 00:22:15,430
- Terrifying.
- Huh?

374
00:22:15,430 --> 00:22:17,880
You're doing great. Now, repeat after me.

375
00:22:17,880 --> 00:22:19,750
- Iroha.
- Iroha.

376
00:22:19,750 --> 00:22:21,250
- Saya.
- Saya.

377
00:22:21,250 --> 00:22:22,460
- Ikuru.
- Ikuru.

378
00:22:22,460 --> 00:22:23,800
- Hey!
- Me.

379
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,240
- Me.
- You.

380
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:26,370
- You.
- That pleb.

381
00:22:26,370 --> 00:22:28,340
- That pleb.
- What are you teaching her?

382
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:30,330
And one more thing. Here.

383
00:22:30,330 --> 00:22:31,510
For good measure.

384
00:22:31,510 --> 00:22:34,490
It's something I used in elementary school.

385
00:22:35,330 --> 00:22:37,390
I registered my number,

386
00:22:37,390 --> 00:22:40,960
so press here, and I'll answer right away.

387
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:42,250
No matter where we are?

388
00:22:42,250 --> 00:22:46,570
Yup. And no matter how far apart we are.

389
00:22:49,640 --> 00:22:52,800
So couriers aren't needed anymore.

390
00:22:54,180 --> 00:22:58,420
If my father saw this, he'd go red with rage.

391
00:22:58,420 --> 00:23:01,160
Well, uh...

392
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:03,230
But some things never change, do they?

393
00:23:03,230 --> 00:23:06,540
People want to be connected,
no matter the age.

394
00:23:06,540 --> 00:23:07,860
Exactly!

395
00:23:07,860 --> 00:23:11,260
So don't be scared, okay?

396
00:23:11,260 --> 00:23:13,060
Come here, I'll tie your hair.

397
00:23:13,060 --> 00:23:15,290
Thanks.

398
00:23:18,130 --> 00:23:20,000
We'll be getting on that thing.

399
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:26,600
Woah! What the hell is this?

400
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:28,970
<i>The doors are now closing.</i>

401
00:23:31,890 --> 00:23:33,790
Ms. Nashiki, over here.

402
00:23:33,790 --> 00:23:35,320
Thanks.

403
00:23:42,170 --> 00:23:44,250
Yo. Did you eat breakfast?

404
00:23:44,250 --> 00:23:45,660
- Pardon?
- Sorry!

405
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:47,450
No talking to strangers.

406
00:23:47,450 --> 00:23:49,800
- Why not?
- Because it's weird!

407
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:51,510
Why?

408
00:23:51,510 --> 00:23:54,760
- Is making conversation weird?
- Just shut up! Shush!

409
00:23:56,760 --> 00:23:58,390
Sorry.

410
00:24:08,450 --> 00:24:10,200
Here we are.

411
00:24:12,970 --> 00:24:15,670
It's huge!

412
00:24:15,670 --> 00:24:18,050
Our school has three classes per year,

413
00:24:18,050 --> 00:24:22,610
and only 459 students, despite being
smack-dab in the middle of Shibuya.

414
00:24:22,610 --> 00:24:25,970
459 students?

415
00:24:28,720 --> 00:24:30,550
I'll be fine.

416
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:34,600
I've been dreaming of this day for ages.

417
00:24:36,410 --> 00:24:37,440
Let's go!

418
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:39,370
W-Wait!

419
00:24:40,300 --> 00:24:42,860
- I'll explain this at the very least.
- Okay.

420
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:45,800
School is an organization,
and hierarchy is its lifeblood.

421
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:46,820
- Organization?
- Yes.

422
00:24:46,820 --> 00:24:49,610
From the top, there's the Principal,
Vice Principal, Head of School Affairs,

423
00:24:49,610 --> 00:24:51,190
Head of Year, and homeroom teachers.

424
00:24:51,190 --> 00:24:54,530
Then separately, there's the Ministry,
Board of Education, and PTA.

425
00:24:54,530 --> 00:24:57,320
- Wait...
- Okay, how about this?

426
00:24:57,320 --> 00:25:00,380
Principal equals daimyo.
Vice principal, the chief retainer.

427
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:03,240
Head of School Affairs, the steward.

428
00:25:03,240 --> 00:25:06,990
Head of Year is a commander.

429
00:25:06,990 --> 00:25:09,200
While homeroom teachers are overseers.

430
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:11,440
And there are also outside powers.

431
00:25:11,440 --> 00:25:13,700
The Ministry is the shogunate.

432
00:25:13,700 --> 00:25:15,780
- The shogunate?
- That's right.

433
00:25:15,780 --> 00:25:16,870
- Good morning.
- Morning.

434
00:25:16,870 --> 00:25:19,430
- Good morning.
- Morning.

435
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:24,910
Yikes, Ms. Sakurai is coming.
Ms. Nashiki, hurry!

436
00:25:26,270 --> 00:25:28,570
The Board of Education are
managers of the daimyo's Edo residence.

437
00:25:28,570 --> 00:25:31,390
The PTA is a gathering
of wealthy merchants, or...

438
00:25:31,390 --> 00:25:33,040
town elders.

439
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:36,010
The children are citizens.
While we teachers are, uh...

440
00:25:36,010 --> 00:25:37,400
retainers!

441
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:39,280
And retainers must obey
the daimyo and shogunate, right?

442
00:25:39,280 --> 00:25:40,900
Of course.

443
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:42,300
Good.

444
00:25:42,950 --> 00:25:45,570
- Ready?
- Yeah.

445
00:25:46,480 --> 00:25:47,870
Good morning.

446
00:25:47,870 --> 00:25:49,620
- Good morning.
- Morning.

447
00:25:49,620 --> 00:25:51,790
- Morning.
- Hey!

448
00:25:51,790 --> 00:25:53,350
Too short a break, huh?

449
00:25:53,350 --> 00:25:56,000
My Golden Week wasn't "golden" at all.

450
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,220
- Good morning.
- Morning.

451
00:25:59,220 --> 00:26:01,110
How many days till summer break?

452
00:26:01,110 --> 00:26:03,430
Come on.

453
00:26:04,330 --> 00:26:09,140
Good morning to you all, fellow comrades!

454
00:26:09,140 --> 00:26:10,650
Huh?

455
00:26:14,380 --> 00:26:16,400
What are you doing? Stand up!

456
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:19,160
- I'm respecting them.
- Really, a joke?

457
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:21,890
A joke, first thing after break?

458
00:26:21,890 --> 00:26:24,900
That wasn't funny at all.

459
00:26:26,570 --> 00:26:29,170
- Principal Tsukamoto! Good morning!
- Good morning.

460
00:26:29,170 --> 00:26:30,370
Madame!

461
00:26:30,370 --> 00:26:32,120
Stop that! Stand!

462
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:34,170
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

463
00:26:34,170 --> 00:26:35,920
- Morning. Morning!
- Alright.

464
00:26:35,920 --> 00:26:38,470
- Golden Week has come and gone once more.
- Hurry.

465
00:26:38,470 --> 00:26:40,480
Sit there. There!

466
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:44,080
Students will avoid school,
and be unmotivated after break.

467
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:49,040
So let us not forget our three core values:

468
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:51,810
Observe, report, and teamwork.

469
00:26:51,810 --> 00:26:54,910
Repeat after me.
Observe, report, and teamwork.

470
00:26:54,910 --> 00:26:57,490
Observe, report, and teamwork.

471
00:26:57,490 --> 00:26:59,560
I don't hear the enthusiasm, folks!

472
00:26:59,560 --> 00:27:00,880
Once again.

473
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:03,080
- <i>Year three, class three.</i>
- Observe, report, and teamwork.

474
00:27:03,080 --> 00:27:05,170
Observe, report, and teamwork.

475
00:27:05,170 --> 00:27:07,490
- Alright!
- Woah!

476
00:27:07,490 --> 00:27:10,170
You're raring to go, Ms. Nashiki.

477
00:27:10,170 --> 00:27:12,170
- Yes, ma'am!
- Ma'am!

478
00:27:12,170 --> 00:27:15,980
- Oh god...
- I wish you all the best of luck.

479
00:27:15,980 --> 00:27:18,940
- Thank you.
- Thank you, ma'am.

480
00:27:21,060 --> 00:27:22,820
Ms. Nashiki.

481
00:27:22,820 --> 00:27:25,120
- What happened?
- Huh?

482
00:27:25,120 --> 00:27:28,080
I mean, you seem very different.

483
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:29,120
What?

484
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:33,880
Ms. Nashiki. Let's keep it simple.
Otherwise we'll all tire out.

485
00:27:33,880 --> 00:27:35,390
Yes, ma'am.

486
00:27:35,990 --> 00:27:38,800
<i>It'll only be a day.</i>

487
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:41,760
Alright, time to head out.

488
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:51,950
<i>One day, and everything will be over.</i>

489
00:27:51,950 --> 00:27:55,000
Good morning!

490
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,390
Hurry, you'll be late!

491
00:27:57,390 --> 00:27:58,950
Your classroom is in the very back.

492
00:27:58,950 --> 00:28:00,450
Right.

493
00:28:01,730 --> 00:28:04,420
- Good morning!
- Morning!

494
00:28:04,420 --> 00:28:05,880
You boys have got gusto.

495
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:07,590
What's with you, Ms. Nashiki?

496
00:28:07,590 --> 00:28:09,360
- Huh?
- No dawdling,

497
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:11,180
you'll be late!

498
00:28:13,290 --> 00:28:15,110
Morning.

499
00:28:15,110 --> 00:28:16,750
Get to your seats, everyone.

500
00:28:16,750 --> 00:28:19,590
Yes, Mr. Majima.

501
00:28:19,590 --> 00:28:21,240
Alright.

502
00:28:21,240 --> 00:28:23,390
Did you all enjoy your Golden Week?

503
00:28:23,390 --> 00:28:25,700
Yes.

504
00:28:25,700 --> 00:28:27,900
I spent break playing soccer with my friends.

505
00:28:27,900 --> 00:28:30,760
- Woah!
- With who?

506
00:28:32,170 --> 00:28:34,170
2 minutes, 16 seconds.

507
00:28:34,170 --> 00:28:37,550
That's how long it took
for you all to settle down.

508
00:28:37,550 --> 00:28:39,610
- Mr. Takagi.
- Yes, Ms. Akutsu?

509
00:28:39,610 --> 00:28:42,820
What do you have to say
about wasting everyone's time?

510
00:28:43,730 --> 00:28:45,090
I'm sorry.

511
00:28:45,090 --> 00:28:46,440
And you, Mr. Mori.

512
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:49,690
If you're so sorry, why do it at all?

513
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:08,650
Good morning!

514
00:29:13,090 --> 00:29:15,620
Good morning!

515
00:29:47,300 --> 00:29:49,900
"To subdue one's self
and return to propriety, is perfect virtue".

516
00:29:49,900 --> 00:29:51,190
- Huh?
- What?

517
00:29:51,190 --> 00:29:53,040
These are the words of Confucius.

518
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:58,610
Propriety is not a means to protect oneself
from outside forces.

519
00:29:58,610 --> 00:30:02,760
Rather, it is a way
to discipline one's heart.

520
00:30:02,760 --> 00:30:05,650
One does not conform for the sake of others.

521
00:30:05,650 --> 00:30:10,660
One conforms to reform oneself.

522
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:13,000
- What's wrong with her?
- What?

523
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:17,420
I look forward to teaching you all.

524
00:30:19,130 --> 00:30:20,520
What's going on?

525
00:30:20,520 --> 00:30:23,330
She's being weird.

526
00:30:25,210 --> 00:30:26,920
To call my break eventful

527
00:30:26,920 --> 00:30:29,710
is quite the understatement.

528
00:30:29,710 --> 00:30:33,920
So while it may seem strange,

529
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:36,120
I ask you all to place these on your desks.

530
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:38,410
Isn't Ms. Nashiki acting weird?

531
00:30:38,410 --> 00:30:41,020
Come get your papers as I read them out.

532
00:30:41,980 --> 00:30:43,730
Makoto Aizawa.

533
00:30:43,730 --> 00:30:45,370
You must be Kenjiro.

534
00:30:45,370 --> 00:30:47,280
Nice to meet you.

535
00:30:48,170 --> 00:30:50,050
Great.

536
00:30:50,050 --> 00:30:52,670
Now I can associate every face with a name.

537
00:30:52,670 --> 00:30:54,550
Ms. Nashiki, you meanie!

538
00:30:54,550 --> 00:30:58,360
My name isn't "Mariso", it's "Marin Jain"!

539
00:30:58,360 --> 00:30:59,970
She wrote your name wrong!

540
00:30:59,970 --> 00:31:02,360
"Mariso Jaiso"? What a weird name.

541
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:03,680
Sounds like a rascal's name.

542
00:31:03,680 --> 00:31:06,770
- Yeah!
- Boys, don't say that!

543
00:31:06,770 --> 00:31:09,020
Silence!

544
00:31:16,450 --> 00:31:18,520
My deepest apologies to you.

545
00:31:18,520 --> 00:31:23,370
Getting a name wrong
is an unforgivable crime.

546
00:31:23,370 --> 00:31:25,850
Ms. Nashiki?

547
00:31:25,850 --> 00:31:28,820
Let me be honest with you.

548
00:31:28,820 --> 00:31:30,640
I need you all to listen as well.

549
00:31:30,640 --> 00:31:31,890
Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

550
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:33,840
Truth be told,

551
00:31:33,840 --> 00:31:36,320
I do badly with katakana.
(T/N: A writing style)

552
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:37,940
What?

553
00:31:37,940 --> 00:31:39,780
Really? With katakana?

554
00:31:39,780 --> 00:31:43,450
Yeah. I never really used it before.

555
00:31:43,450 --> 00:31:45,100
- What?
- How?

556
00:31:45,100 --> 00:31:47,960
I haven't had enough practice with it.

557
00:31:50,720 --> 00:31:53,780
These ones especially give me trouble.

558
00:31:54,880 --> 00:31:56,380
"So"...

559
00:31:56,380 --> 00:31:57,740
And?

560
00:31:57,740 --> 00:31:58,780
"N".

561
00:31:58,780 --> 00:32:00,800
These ones, too.

562
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:03,930
"Shi".

563
00:32:03,930 --> 00:32:05,200
And...

564
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:07,650
- "Tsu"?
- And also...

565
00:32:07,650 --> 00:32:08,940
And hiragana.

566
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:11,860
Now that's an entirely separate beast.
[Yu]

567
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:14,400
Knock it off, Ms. Nashiki.
[Yu]

568
00:32:18,730 --> 00:32:20,920
The katakana script...

569
00:32:23,960 --> 00:32:26,410
derives from Chinese characters.

570
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:29,880
Woah.

571
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:31,780
You there, you're smart.

572
00:32:31,780 --> 00:32:34,760
- You could use my name.
- Right.

573
00:32:34,760 --> 00:32:36,530
Sorry about that...

574
00:32:39,160 --> 00:32:42,220
Ichika.

575
00:32:42,220 --> 00:32:44,650
Then what about "wo"?

576
00:32:44,650 --> 00:32:47,570
What Chinese character does that come from?

577
00:32:47,570 --> 00:32:49,450
I... I don't know.

578
00:32:49,450 --> 00:32:52,340
Hey, I want to know, too!

579
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:55,280
Pipe down! Do your worksheets!

580
00:32:55,280 --> 00:32:58,220
Your worksheets! Sit down.

581
00:33:00,880 --> 00:33:02,650
What?

582
00:33:03,630 --> 00:33:04,990
Hey, wait up!

583
00:33:04,990 --> 00:33:09,030
Where are you guys going?

584
00:33:10,180 --> 00:33:14,090
Over here? Hey, wait!

585
00:33:30,370 --> 00:33:33,740
Struggle to complete it, and you never will.

586
00:33:33,740 --> 00:33:36,940
Fun is the path to knowledge.

587
00:33:36,940 --> 00:33:38,910
Yes, Master.

588
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:42,350
Ms. Nashiki, hurry!

589
00:33:42,350 --> 00:33:43,820
- Be right there.
- Hurry!

590
00:33:43,820 --> 00:33:45,380
Alright.

591
00:33:45,380 --> 00:33:47,380
Found something already?

592
00:33:58,800 --> 00:34:00,060
What're you guys up to?

593
00:34:00,060 --> 00:34:03,220
Look, we found something interesting!

594
00:34:03,220 --> 00:34:04,330
Look here.

595
00:34:04,330 --> 00:34:05,610
Apparently, "sa" is from "san"!

596
00:34:05,610 --> 00:34:08,140
- Really?
- No way.

597
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:10,060
How does "he" come from "bu"?

598
00:34:10,060 --> 00:34:11,470
It's read as "oozato".

599
00:34:11,470 --> 00:34:13,990
But how does "oozato"

600
00:34:13,990 --> 00:34:15,250
- become "he"?
- Ms. Nashiki, look!

601
00:34:15,250 --> 00:34:17,310
"Te" comes from "ten"!

602
00:34:17,310 --> 00:34:19,490
You found a good one, Hinata!

603
00:34:19,490 --> 00:34:21,860
Everyone, listen up!

604
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:24,430
I heard a commotion over in her class.

605
00:34:24,430 --> 00:34:26,550
She's been acting strange all day today.

606
00:34:26,550 --> 00:34:29,930
I heard something about the library as well.

607
00:34:29,930 --> 00:34:31,480
What are you doing?

608
00:34:31,480 --> 00:34:34,370
Nothing. Just admiring the weather.

609
00:34:34,370 --> 00:34:36,010
I see. Mind if I get past you there?

610
00:34:36,010 --> 00:34:38,180
- How's your health?
- Fine.

611
00:34:38,180 --> 00:34:40,850
Excuse me.

612
00:34:40,850 --> 00:34:42,710
Class 3?

613
00:34:42,710 --> 00:34:45,630
Look above, and you'll always see it.

614
00:34:45,630 --> 00:34:47,000
- What's that?
- The sky!

615
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:49,510
Exactly. The sky.

616
00:34:49,510 --> 00:34:53,430
And that celestial body up there
uses the same character.

617
00:34:53,430 --> 00:34:55,230
Celestial body?

618
00:34:55,230 --> 00:34:58,120
- I think she means the sun.
- You got it.

619
00:34:58,120 --> 00:35:01,040
- The sun is the same...
- What are you kids doing?

620
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:02,180
It's recess right now.

621
00:35:02,180 --> 00:35:05,370
Ms. Akutsu!
I'm interested in Chinese characters now!

622
00:35:05,370 --> 00:35:09,820
It's like I'm learning brand new characters,
even though I'm not!

623
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:16,660
Huh? That's the bell to end recess.

624
00:35:17,740 --> 00:35:19,870
- What's up?
- Hurry!

625
00:35:19,870 --> 00:35:21,470
- Why? Where's everyone going?
- Ms. Nashiki, come on!

626
00:35:21,470 --> 00:35:23,230
Alright, I'm coming.

627
00:35:23,230 --> 00:35:25,860
Don't forget your things!

628
00:35:25,860 --> 00:35:28,310
No running in the halls!

629
00:35:29,880 --> 00:35:32,270
Ms. Nashiki.

630
00:35:32,270 --> 00:35:36,150
Where did you discover
this method of teaching?

631
00:35:36,150 --> 00:35:38,020
- Huh?
- I mean,

632
00:35:38,020 --> 00:35:40,460
it's quite... unique.

633
00:35:40,460 --> 00:35:43,870
You must make sure to separate
recess and class time.

634
00:35:43,870 --> 00:35:45,340
It messes with discipline.

635
00:35:45,340 --> 00:35:47,920
Yes, ma'am. Sorry.

636
00:35:52,190 --> 00:35:54,740
Aw, man.

637
00:36:13,290 --> 00:36:16,050
<i>She taught a class.</i>

638
00:36:16,050 --> 00:36:18,820
<i>She taught, using her own methods.</i>

639
00:36:21,740 --> 00:36:23,810
Wanna go to the park?

640
00:36:23,810 --> 00:36:24,990
Aw, I wanna play indoors.

641
00:36:24,990 --> 00:36:28,030
I don't think you can come over today.

642
00:36:30,140 --> 00:36:32,350
We're 2-to-3 now.

643
00:36:32,350 --> 00:36:34,530
- Yeah.
- Dude, watch out.

644
00:36:34,530 --> 00:36:36,650
You guys are in the way.

645
00:36:39,030 --> 00:36:44,710
<i>He touched her! He touched her!</i>

646
00:36:44,710 --> 00:36:46,360
<i>He touched her!</i>
[Not my problem]

647
00:36:49,260 --> 00:36:50,710
What sort of hit was that?

648
00:36:50,710 --> 00:36:52,470
Try again.

649
00:37:03,650 --> 00:37:06,510
Hey, you two. Stop.

650
00:37:06,510 --> 00:37:08,320
We're not bothering anyone.

651
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,320
- Hey, that was scary.
- You're already fourth graders.

652
00:37:10,320 --> 00:37:12,140
It's fine, Mr. Ichinose.

653
00:37:12,140 --> 00:37:14,150
Quit messing around.

654
00:37:15,080 --> 00:37:16,790
Get cleaning, you two.

655
00:37:16,790 --> 00:37:18,750
Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

656
00:37:20,180 --> 00:37:21,650
- Stop it.
- It's fine,

657
00:37:21,650 --> 00:37:23,430
- shut up already.
- You'll hurt someone.

658
00:37:23,430 --> 00:37:25,740
It'll be fine.

659
00:37:30,890 --> 00:37:33,130
- Ready?
- Yeah.

660
00:37:37,990 --> 00:37:40,130
Enough!

661
00:37:44,420 --> 00:37:46,250
Watch out!

662
00:37:55,360 --> 00:37:57,150
Are you okay?

663
00:37:57,810 --> 00:38:00,610
Are you okay? You aren't hurt?

664
00:38:00,610 --> 00:38:02,040
I'm so sorry.

665
00:38:02,040 --> 00:38:04,410
- Are you hurt?
- What's wrong?

666
00:38:05,230 --> 00:38:07,300
Oh crap.

667
00:38:09,020 --> 00:38:10,770
Hey.

668
00:38:14,270 --> 00:38:16,080
- Get over here.
- Let me go!

669
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:18,580
I'm so sorry.

670
00:38:18,580 --> 00:38:22,880
We'll have to tell her parents the truth,

671
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:24,710
and apologize profusely to them.

672
00:38:24,710 --> 00:38:27,730
Right. The responsibility was mine.

673
00:38:27,730 --> 00:38:30,220
I will go apologize to them.

674
00:38:30,220 --> 00:38:36,250
In this case,
a student accidentally flung a broom,

675
00:38:36,250 --> 00:38:39,750
causing a window to shatter.

676
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:46,500
Strictly speaking, it's not your fault.

677
00:38:48,210 --> 00:38:53,910
However, due to issues you faced
at your previous schools...

678
00:38:56,060 --> 00:39:02,110
I don't believe you caused any harassment,
or used physical punishment.

679
00:39:03,610 --> 00:39:06,050
I believe every incident
was a misunderstanding.

680
00:39:06,050 --> 00:39:08,560
You are a serious individual, after all.

681
00:39:09,530 --> 00:39:11,050
Which is exactly why I ask

682
00:39:11,050 --> 00:39:16,280
that you pull yourself together,
so you are not misunderstood again.

683
00:39:21,570 --> 00:39:23,700
Thank you.

684
00:39:30,770 --> 00:39:32,910
What?

685
00:39:32,910 --> 00:39:36,010
Why do you have to apologize?

686
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:44,410
You two...

687
00:39:44,410 --> 00:39:47,330
This incident was their fault alone.

688
00:39:50,520 --> 00:39:52,490
- Hey, stop!
- Let go!

689
00:39:52,490 --> 00:39:54,510
What's your deal?
You teach third grade, not us!

690
00:39:54,510 --> 00:39:56,240
Silence!

691
00:39:58,450 --> 00:40:01,150
"Clean, handle, conduct".

692
00:40:01,150 --> 00:40:05,820
To clean. To handle people. To know conduct.

693
00:40:05,820 --> 00:40:10,700
These are the three pillars
of being human.

694
00:40:10,700 --> 00:40:12,110
Being human?

695
00:40:12,110 --> 00:40:18,350
Disregarding discipline can be life or death
in a warrior household.

696
00:40:19,490 --> 00:40:22,960
Those who do not properly use their tools,

697
00:40:22,960 --> 00:40:26,050
are punished for their sloppy nature.

698
00:40:26,050 --> 00:40:28,920
Brooms, sickles,

699
00:40:28,920 --> 00:40:31,970
both transform,
depending on their uses, like so.

700
00:40:39,110 --> 00:40:40,680
We're sorry.

701
00:40:40,680 --> 00:40:42,170
We're sorry.

702
00:40:42,170 --> 00:40:43,610
What?

703
00:40:43,610 --> 00:40:46,760
The teachers aren't to blame.

704
00:40:46,760 --> 00:40:49,870
Their parents ought to teach them
better discipline.

705
00:40:49,870 --> 00:40:51,790
Ms. Nashiki.

706
00:40:59,610 --> 00:41:03,110
Suzuki. Kudo.

707
00:41:03,110 --> 00:41:05,340
Thanks for apologizing.

708
00:41:06,560 --> 00:41:08,680
Be careful in the halls from now on, okay?

709
00:41:12,230 --> 00:41:14,070
You two can go now.

710
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:21,200
Why? You won't tell their parents?

711
00:41:21,200 --> 00:41:23,240
Don't meddle any further.

712
00:41:24,800 --> 00:41:27,620
What you say is right.

713
00:41:27,620 --> 00:41:31,910
But nowadays, what's factually right

714
00:41:31,910 --> 00:41:35,780
doesn't necessarily come across
as being correct.

715
00:41:38,110 --> 00:41:40,940
Teachers are never just.

716
00:41:47,240 --> 00:41:49,230
<i>So you had no class today?</i>

717
00:41:49,230 --> 00:41:50,830
No, we did.

718
00:41:50,830 --> 00:41:53,450
- We went to the library, and...
- That's self-studying.

719
00:41:53,450 --> 00:41:55,170
Did your teacher not teach?

720
00:41:55,170 --> 00:41:56,780
No, Mom. She...

721
00:41:56,780 --> 00:42:00,060
Preparation for middle school exams
start in third grade.

722
00:42:00,060 --> 00:42:03,150
I wonder if the other moms know of this.

723
00:42:03,730 --> 00:42:07,330
Daichi. You'll be late for cram school.

724
00:42:07,330 --> 00:42:09,510
- Okay.
- I left your bag there.

725
00:42:09,510 --> 00:42:11,200
Thanks.

726
00:42:11,810 --> 00:42:14,790
[Did you hear about Ms. Nashiki today?]
[She's being so irresponsible.]

727
00:42:35,560 --> 00:42:37,220
Ikuru?

728
00:42:38,550 --> 00:42:40,220
Hey, Ikuru!

729
00:42:43,780 --> 00:42:46,800
I wasn't waiting for you.

730
00:42:53,480 --> 00:42:55,610
Hey.

731
00:42:55,610 --> 00:42:57,580
What did you mean by that earlier?

732
00:42:58,780 --> 00:43:01,620
About teachers not ever being just?

733
00:43:03,870 --> 00:43:05,320
I meant it quite literally.

734
00:43:05,320 --> 00:43:09,450
Like how a huge bonfire
can start from nothing.

735
00:43:09,450 --> 00:43:10,910
That doesn't make sense.

736
00:43:10,910 --> 00:43:13,250
Obviously someone set the flame first.

737
00:43:13,250 --> 00:43:15,110
I'm sure there is a culprit.

738
00:43:15,110 --> 00:43:17,950
But what use is there in exposing them?

739
00:43:17,950 --> 00:43:20,680
The culprits are the student's parents.

740
00:43:20,680 --> 00:43:23,070
You're lucky, Ms. Iroha.

741
00:43:23,070 --> 00:43:27,180
You know a time
where teachers are respected above all.

742
00:43:27,860 --> 00:43:29,680
Are you saying it's different now?

743
00:43:29,680 --> 00:43:31,740
Unfortunately, yes.

744
00:43:31,740 --> 00:43:35,700
To be frank with you,
my kids look down on me.

745
00:43:35,700 --> 00:43:39,140
I can't argue against their parents either.

746
00:43:39,140 --> 00:43:43,900
Then why did you become a teacher?

747
00:43:45,610 --> 00:43:49,030
<i>How can you prepare yourself
for what's to come every day?</i>

748
00:43:49,030 --> 00:43:53,760
<i>Isn't it because you want to become
your version of an ideal teacher?</i>

749
00:43:53,760 --> 00:43:55,560
<i>I suppose so.</i>

750
00:43:55,560 --> 00:43:58,450
<i>But if I only followed my ideals,</i>

751
00:43:58,450 --> 00:44:01,030
I'd never get anywhere.

752
00:44:01,030 --> 00:44:05,530
In the game of life,
I'd have negative points.

753
00:44:06,460 --> 00:44:10,820
That's why I choose to accomplish
the bare minimum every day.

754
00:44:10,820 --> 00:44:13,680
The bare minimum?

755
00:44:13,680 --> 00:44:17,710
To protect the lives of my students.

756
00:44:21,290 --> 00:44:22,740
It's not a grand thing, really.

757
00:44:22,740 --> 00:44:25,470
Modern technology may make things convenient,

758
00:44:25,470 --> 00:44:29,060
but that doesn't mean
life is any easier to live.

759
00:44:31,250 --> 00:44:34,730
That's why I want to protect the lives
of not only my own students,

760
00:44:34,730 --> 00:44:40,110
<i>but all students of Shibuya Third,
including those who don't attend school.</i>

761
00:44:40,110 --> 00:44:45,680
I guess that's my absolute,
ultimate goal I won't budge on.

762
00:44:53,470 --> 00:44:57,610
[Come, and you have a date.]
[You have 15 minutes.]

763
00:45:00,410 --> 00:45:03,940
Sorry, I bet that sounded nerdy.

764
00:45:03,940 --> 00:45:05,890
"N-Nerdy"?

765
00:45:05,890 --> 00:45:08,840
Absolute, ultimate... Pretty cringe, right?

766
00:45:08,840 --> 00:45:10,600
Cringe?

767
00:45:10,600 --> 00:45:13,130
Is that a bad thing?

768
00:45:13,130 --> 00:45:16,510
Yes. It's a terrible thing
to be afflicted with.

769
00:45:16,510 --> 00:45:18,930
Being cringe can ruin your life.

770
00:45:18,930 --> 00:45:20,350
But enough about me.

771
00:45:20,350 --> 00:45:22,120
Huh?

772
00:45:22,120 --> 00:45:24,070
I didn't get a word you said.

773
00:45:24,070 --> 00:45:26,970
Neither did I.

774
00:45:27,810 --> 00:45:30,550
But you know,

775
00:45:30,550 --> 00:45:32,490
you're a cool guy.

776
00:45:33,910 --> 00:45:35,380
Huh?

777
00:45:36,510 --> 00:45:38,350
You're cool.

778
00:45:39,180 --> 00:45:40,670
Huh?

779
00:45:56,400 --> 00:46:00,830
<i>Please leave a message after the tone.</i>

780
00:46:01,640 --> 00:46:04,550
Ikuru, are you still at school?

781
00:46:04,550 --> 00:46:07,070
Or...

782
00:46:07,070 --> 00:46:09,450
<i>Are you with someone?</i>

783
00:46:09,450 --> 00:46:12,050
<i>"Cool"?</i>

784
00:46:15,030 --> 00:46:16,940
<i>Me?</i>
